# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore — what never enters the build context
# =============================================================================
# Before Docker runs a single instruction it TARS THIS DIRECTORY and ships it to
# the daemon. Without this file that tarball includes node_modules (~300 MB),
# dist, coverage and the whole git history — so every build begins with a
# multi-second upload of files that are about to be thrown away or, worse,
# copied in.
#
# THE CORRECTNESS HALF matters more than the speed half: excluding node_modules
# is what makes the multi-stage build honest. Copy the host's tree in and a
# Linux image can end up running native modules built for Windows — and the
# failure appears at runtime, not at build time.
# =============================================================================

# Reinstalled inside the image by `npm ci`, for the target platform.
node_modules

# Rebuilt by `npm run build`. Copying a stale host build is the classic
# "why is my fix not in the image" bug.
dist
coverage

# History, including anything ever committed and later removed.
.git
.gitignore

# SECRETS. Nothing here is a runtime secret for a static SPA — Vite bakes
# VITE_* values into the bundle at BUILD time, so they are public by
# construction. Excluded anyway: a .env.local pointing at a staging API would
# silently change what the image contains.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Prose. Excluding it means editing a doc does not invalidate the build cache.
docs
*.md

# Tooling that plays no part in the build.
.vscode
.idea
eslint.config.js
.prettierrc
.prettierignore

# The OpenAPI snapshot is a development artefact — `npm run api:types`
# regenerates it. The TYPES it produced are in src/ and do ship.
openapi

# Test files. `tsc -b` excludes them from the build output already; excluding
# them from the CONTEXT means editing a test does not bust the Docker cache
# either.
src/**/*.test.ts
src/**/*.test.tsx
src/test
